 Let's denote the first term of the arithmetic series as $a$ and the common difference as $d$.  
The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic series is given by
\begin{equation*}
S_n=\frac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d)
\end{equation*}
Therefore, for the first 5 terms:
\begin{equation*}
S_5=\frac{5}{2}(2a+(5-1)d)=70
\end{equation*}
And for the first 10 terms:
\begin{equation*}
S_{10}=\frac{10}{2}(2a+(10-1)d)=210
\end{equation*}
Simplifying these equations:
\begin{equation*}
5a+10d=70 \tag{1}\label{equation1}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
10a+45d=210 \tag{2}\label{equation2}
\end{equation*}
To solve this system of linear equations, we can multiply equation \eqref{equation1} by 2 and subtract it from equation \eqref{equation2}:
\begin{align*}
2(5a+10d)&=2(70)\\
\Rightarrow 10a+20d&=140
\end{align*}
Subtracting this from equation \eqref{equation2}:
\begin{align*}
(10a+45d)-(10a+20d)&=210-140\\
\Rightarrow 25d&=70
\end{align*}
Thus:
\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{70}{25}=\boxed{\frac{14}{5}}
\end{equation*}
Substituting $d$ back into equation \eqref{equation1}:
\begin{equation*}
5a+10\left(\frac{14}{5}\right)=70
\end{equation*}
Simplifying:
\begin{equation*}
5a+28=70
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
5a=42
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
a=\boxed{\frac{42}{5}}
\end{equation*}
